# G073 — Telnet Application

*v2. See `telnet.md` for prose: protocol split from transport, session
as a three-state FSM with Disconnected as terminal, commands as
dispatch map, state-gated commands as security policy, per-session
history as journal, sessions isolated across callers.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Agent Command REPL

[agent-repl
    IN ORDER TO
        expose agent operations over a line-oriented protocol
        where authentication gates sensitive commands
        and each client holds its own session independent of any other

    @srv <- [telnet[new-server]]
    [telnet[register {srv: @srv, name: "deploy",
                       handler: [agent[deploy-handler]],
                       legal-states: ["Authenticated"]}]]
    [telnet[register {srv: @srv, name: "rollback",
                       handler: [agent[rollback-handler]],
                       legal-states: ["Authenticated"]}]]

    sync @socket-events {
        accept @conn ->
            @sid <- [telnet[connect {srv: @srv}]]
            loop
                @line <- [@conn[read-line]]
                @out  <- [telnet[handle-line
                                   {srv: @srv, sid: @sid, line: @line}]]
                [@conn[write-line {text: @out:text}]]
                @out where kind is "disconnect" -> break
    }
]

---

## Verb Surface

[telnet
    new-server
    register         {srv, name, handler, legal-states}
    connect          {srv}                         ;; -> session-id
    handle-line      {srv, sid, line}              ;; -> {kind, text}
    history          {srv, sid}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: state gate as `where` access policy

[telnet[handle-line {srv: @srv, sid: @sid, line: @line}]
    IN ORDER TO
        dispatch @line's command only when the session's state allows it
        so the access-control surface is visible as a table, not buried in ifs

    @session <- @srv:sessions at @sid
        where state is "Disconnected"
            -> #session-terminated (refuse all commands)
    @cmd <- parse @line
    @handler <- @srv:commands at @cmd:name
        where @session:state is in @handler:legal-states
            -> run @handler with @cmd:args
        otherwise
            -> #not-allowed-in-state (do not reveal whether command exists)
]

`help` works in any state. `login` only in Unauthenticated. `whoami`
and `who` only in Authenticated. The legal-in-state table IS the
protocol's security policy; every historical protocol bug has been
a command accidentally legal in the wrong state.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: per-session history as audit journal

[telnet[history {srv: @srv, sid: @sid}]
    @session:history is append-only
    where each entry is (line, reply) recorded after dispatch
    IN ORDER TO
        serve as an audit trail, not a replay mechanism
        so if state ever desyncs the journal is the recovery source
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    @srv <- [telnet[new-server]]
    @s1 <- [telnet[connect {srv: @srv}]]
    @s2 <- [telnet[connect {srv: @srv}]]

    -> [telnet[handle-line
                 {srv: @srv, sid: @s1, line: "whoami"}]]
    ;; -> #not-allowed-in-state (Unauthenticated)

    -> [telnet[handle-line
                 {srv: @srv, sid: @s1, line: "login alice"}]]
    ;; -> {kind: "ok", text: "welcome alice"}

    -> [telnet[handle-line
                 {srv: @srv, sid: @s1, line: "whoami"}]]
    ;; -> {kind: "ok", text: "alice"}

    -> [telnet[handle-line
                 {srv: @srv, sid: @s2, line: "whoami"}]]
    ;; -> #not-allowed-in-state (s2 unaffected by s1's login — isolation)
]
